#
# The same closure census as tests/conformance-fixtures/enactment-closure-predicate-census.ttl,
# with exactly one thing removed: the budget the fixed point was proved under.
#
# This is the failure mode a content-free witness cannot illustrate. A witness that
# names nothing is obviously worthless once anyone looks; this one names one hundred
# and fifty-nine settled predicates and looks like the most thorough evidence in the
# system. But a fixed point is a claim RELATIVE to a bound — "no further rule
# application adds a tuple" is only meaningful alongside how many applications were
# tried — so an enumeration with no budget records what the chase happened to reach,
# never that it had nowhere left to go. Every predicate on the list would appear in
# exactly the same position if the search had been cut off one step later than the
# longest chain it happened to explore.
#
# The volume is the hazard, not an accident of the fixture: the longer the
# enumeration, the less likely a reviewer is to notice the one missing line.
#
# MUST trip logic:FrontierClosureRequiresSaturationConstraint — the law that READS
# the witness rather than counting it. logic:FrontierCarriesSaturationWitnessConstraint
# passes here, because a witness is present and does carry content.

@prefix logic: <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/> .
@prefix rdfs:  <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd:   <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix ex:    <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/examples/logic/tests/> .

ex:unboundedCensusFrontier a logic:ActionableFrontier ;
    rdfs:label "a frontier claiming closure from an enumeration with no budget"@en ;
    logic:frontierSaturationWitness ex:unboundedCensusSaturation .

ex:unboundedCensusSaturation a logic:SaturationWitness ;
    rdfs:label "a saturation witness that enumerates everything and bounds nothing"@en ;
    logic:resultEvaluation logic:EvaluationCompleted ;
    logic:settledPredicate
